training-agents-into-weights-lines-12 : List (List Word)
training-agents-into-weights-lines-12 = cons "- **Whole-argument training examples.** Months of sustained technical writing and dialogues pair premises, derivations, and conclusions inside single turns. Each training example preserves the complete message intact, because truncating to arbitrary chunk lengths severs conclusions from the arguments that establish them." (cons "- **Natural supervision from interaction transcripts.** Every assistant turn in the historical archive is followed immediately by its human evaluation — accepted, refined, or rejected with an exact diagnosis of the error. Tens of thousands of labeled turns already exist, allowing a learned evaluator of architectural and epistemic fidelity to score candidate generations offline." (cons "- **On-policy rejected samples from the target open model.** Because an open-weight base model exhibits different failure modes from commercial frontier models, rejected preference pairs are sampled by placing the open model itself into real repository states and grading its unaligned defaults against the formal specification and core paper." (cons "- **Continuous training as a maintained view.** Every training pair is keyed by the content hashes of the store objects from which it was derived; new interactions and edits enter the store as new content-addressed objects, regenerating only downstream training shards whose dependency certificates changed (Part I, §10.2 and §10.5). The resulting LoRA adapter is itself a content-addressed object in the store whose provenance is the exact corpus hash closure." (cons "- **Kernel verification for RL rewards.** The cubical checker (`--serve`) grades formal declarations deterministically with acceptance or type error, turning every construction in the paper and workflow graph into a verifiable reinforcement-learning environment rewarded strictly on first-attempt validity." (cons "" nil)))))
